    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Defense Evasion expectation / IT Breach Response", "technique": "N/A — remediation action", "what_happened": "At 2026-05-13 10:00:31 CEST, Wazuh's Azure AD / Office365 module logged an AuditLogs UserManagement event: activity=ResetUserPassword, initiatedBy=it-admin01@corp.com, targetUser=employee07@corp.onmicrosoft.com, reason 'account compromise suspected', ticket INC-2026-05-01-88, result=success. This is a legitimate IT breach-response action — the SOC/IT team reset the password of an account they suspected was compromised, on the assumption that a password reset locks the attacker out. Wazuh fired a baseline informational rule for the password-reset audit event.", "chain_reason": "rule.groups contained 'office365'/'azure' and rule.description matched 'Reset user password' / 'ResetUserPassword' for targetUser employee07@corp.onmicrosoft.com. On its own this is a benign administrative event — it becomes the anchor of the chain only because of what follows it.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:00:31+0200"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence / Use Alternate Authentication Material — Application Access Token", "technique": "T1550.001", "what_happened": "At 10:02:14 CEST — only ~1m43s AFTER the password reset — Wazuh's SignInLogs feed logged a non-interactive token redemption: appDisplayName=MailSyncPro, user=employee07@corp.onmicrosoft.com, tokenType=Refresh, grantType=refresh_token, callerIpAddress=10.108.57.184, isInteractive=false, result=Success. The OAuth refresh token held by the MailSyncPro app was used to mint a fresh access token — and it SUCCEEDED, despite the password having been reset 103 seconds earlier. This is the core T1550.001 signal: OAuth refresh tokens are credential-independent; a password reset does NOT revoke them unless the admin explicitly calls revokeSignInSessions. Wazuh fired rule 99210 ('Azure AD: API activity after password reset') and rule 99211 ('Azure AD: Refresh token not revoked on reset').", "chain_reason": "rule.id 99210/99211 fired with rule.description matching 'API activity after password reset' / 'Refresh token not revoked on reset', referencing user employee07@corp.onmicrosoft.com and app MailSyncPro, time-correlated within a 10-minute span of the step-1 ResetUserPassword event. The grantType=refresh_token + isInteractive=false + result=Success after a password reset is the canonical token-survives-reset signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:14+0200"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Collection / Email Collection — Remote Email Collection", "technique": "T1114.002", "what_happened": "From 10:02:40 to 10:07:30 CEST, the MailSyncPro app — still authenticated by the surviving refresh token — drove a burst of Microsoft Graph API calls for employee07: GetMessages with deltaParam=true (incremental mailbox sync), then GetMessages with topParam=1000 and selectParam=body,attachments,from,to,subject pulling ~46 MB (responseBytes=48291840), ListContacts returning 847 rows (full address book), ListCalendarEvents, and GetDriveItems against OneDrive pulling ~120 MB (responseBytes=125829120). All operations: auth_type=OAuth, result=success, caller=MailSyncPro, callerIpAddress=10.108.57.184. Wazuh fired rule 99212 ('Azure AD: Bulk mail read post-compromise'). This is active bulk exfiltration of mail, contacts and files using a token that the SOC believed was already neutralised by the password reset.", "chain_reason": "rule.id 99212 fired with rule.description matching 'Bulk mail read post-compromise' for user employee07@corp.onmicrosoft.com, immediately following the step-2 token redemption from the SAME callerIpAddress=10.108.57.184. The topParam=1000 + selectParam including body/attachments + the OneDrive GetDriveItems volume is the canonical post-compromise bulk-collection signature.", "first_seen": "2026-05-13T10:02:40+0200"}
  ],
